1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:00,836
QwenOS.

2
00:00:00,836 --> 00:00:06,692
A tiny operating system, and the real DOOM engine, running on a computer that lives inside the

3
00:00:06,692 --> 00:00:08,436
weights of a language model.

4
00:00:08,436 --> 00:00:13,046
This is a short, honest tour of what it is and what was actually verified.

5
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:18,924
Qwenputer is a thirty two bit RISC-V processor compiled into the layers of Qwen three point

6
00:00:18,924 --> 00:00:20,531
five, four billion parameters.

7
00:00:20,531 --> 00:00:25,672
Every instruction is a few generated tokens, and the RAM is held in the model's recurrent state.

8
00:00:25,672 --> 00:00:30,760
We wanted to know whether that processor can run a real operating system, and then a real game.

9
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:35,677
On top sits a small kernel and shell in thirty two kibibytes of RAM.

10
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:41,148
The shell starts doomd, which is the DMC100 DOOM engine with Freedoom data, running as ordinary

11
00:00:41,148 --> 00:00:41,839
RISC-V code.

12
00:00:41,839 --> 00:00:46,850
Below that is the Qwenputer circuit inside Qwen three point five, which executes it one

13
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:48,117
instruction at a time.

14
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:51,310
The same program can run three ways.

15
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:53,134
T zero is our fast C emulator.

16
00:00:53,134 --> 00:00:56,905
T one is the upstream reference CPU, which acts as the oracle.

17
00:00:56,905 --> 00:00:59,216
T two is the real Qwen model on a GPU.

18
00:00:59,216 --> 00:01:04,569
In a model run, every token the model produces is compared with the oracle, and a single

19
00:01:04,569 --> 00:01:06,819
mismatch would count as a divergence.

20
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:11,065
The headline result is the three second DOOM clip.

21
00:01:11,065 --> 00:01:14,635
Forty five frames of E1M1 at fifteen frames per second.

22
00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:20,217
Its instruction trace was executed by the Qwen model in 35 separate chunks, 81,065,591

23
00:01:20,217 --> 00:01:24,955
instructions in total, in lockstep with the oracle, with zero divergence.

24
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:29,749
This is the cost of each frame, in millions of instructions.

25
00:01:29,749 --> 00:01:33,484
The first frame includes boot and level load, 3.42 million.

26
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:38,043
After that every frame costs between 1.07 and 2.78 million instructions.

27
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:46,461
The chunks ran in parallel on three lanes: 29 on Modal A100s, 3 on Lambda A100s and 3 on AWS

28
00:01:46,461 --> 00:01:46,945
A10Gs.

29
00:01:46,945 --> 00:01:52,834
Together that is about 21.2 hours of GPU time, for three seconds of game.

30
00:01:53,830 --> 00:02:00,472
A second, separate run typed the commands into the shell: help, ls, and run slash bin slash

31
00:02:00,472 --> 00:02:04,559
doomd, then followed the first three DOOM frame markers.

32
00:02:04,559 --> 00:02:10,252
That is 8,239,190 instructions across 27 segments, again with zero divergence.

33
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,019
You can play with it yourself.

34
00:02:13,019 --> 00:02:17,383
Make live doom boots QwenOS in a terminal and drops you at a shell prompt.

35
00:02:17,383 --> 00:02:22,691
Be clear about what that is: it is the host emulator, tier T zero, running the same kernel

36
00:02:22,691 --> 00:02:24,755
shell and DOOM, not the Qwen model.

37
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:27,624
Now what is real and what is not.

38
00:02:27,624 --> 00:02:32,876
For the clip, the model verified the instruction trace and the frame markers; the pixels were

39
00:02:32,876 --> 00:02:34,852
read out of memory by the emulator.

40
00:02:34,852 --> 00:02:39,822
Keys are scripted, the clock is virtual, the disk is served by the host, and thirty five

41
00:02:39,822 --> 00:02:41,968
boundary instructions run on the host.

42
00:02:41,968 --> 00:02:46,373
And it is nowhere near real time: a displayed frame costs minutes of GPU time.

43
00:02:47,370 --> 00:02:52,878
What it does show is that a real kernel, a shell and the DOOM engine fit into thirty two

44
00:02:52,878 --> 00:02:58,198
kibibytes, and that a cold boot to the first frame marker ran on the model, 1,552,382

45
00:02:58,198 --> 00:03:00,013
instructions in 292 segments.

46
00:03:00,013 --> 00:03:04,833
In every model run listed in the repo, the model's tokens matched the oracle.

47
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:10,200
The code, the per-chunk ledger and the full list of limits are in the repo.

